The Pulumi MCP server is hosted by Pulumi at `https://mcp.ai.pulumi.com/mcp`, so there is nothing to install or run locally. You add that URL to your AI agent, then authenticate with a Pulumi access token. The docs cover Claude Code, Cursor, Claude Desktop, Windsurf, Kiro, and headless setups. You didn't say which agent you use, so here are the main options.

**Claude Code**

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http pulumi https://mcp.ai.pulumi.com/mcp
```

Then run `/mcp` in Claude Code, select **pulumi**, and authenticate in the browser window that opens.

**Cursor**

Add the server to `.cursor/mcp.json` in your home directory or project root:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "pulumi": {
      "url": "https://mcp.ai.pulumi.com/mcp"
    }
  }
}
```

Then select **Connect** next to the server under **Cursor Settings > Tools & MCP** and authenticate in the browser.

**Claude Desktop**

1. Open **Settings > Connectors** and select **Add custom connector**.
2. Set the name to `Pulumi` and the URL to `https://mcp.ai.pulumi.com/mcp`.
3. Restart Claude Desktop and authenticate in the browser when prompted.

**Windsurf**

Add this in **Windsurf Settings > MCP Servers**, then authenticate in the browser when prompted:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "pulumi": {
      "serverUrl": "https://mcp.ai.pulumi.com/mcp"
    }
  }
}
```

**Authentication**

On first connect, the browser flow asks you to paste a Pulumi access token, choose a default organization, and select **Authorize Connection**. The docs recommend a token separate from the one the Pulumi CLI uses, so you can rotate it without affecting the CLI. Sessions last 30 days.

**Headless or CI setups**

To skip the browser flow, send the token in an `Authorization` header and set the organization with `X-Pulumi-Org`:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "pulumi": {
      "url": "https://mcp.ai.pulumi.com/mcp",
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": "Bearer ${PULUMI_MCP_TOKEN}",
        "X-Pulumi-Org": "acme"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Read the token from an environment variable or secret store rather than committing it. If `X-Pulumi-Org` is missing or names an organization the token can't access, the server silently uses the first organization the token can access.

**Neo cost note**

The `neo-bridge` tool can incur Neo usage charges, and approving a Neo request can let Neo change infrastructure. To prevent this, deny `neo-bridge` in your agent's permissions. In Claude Code, add `mcp__pulumi__neo-bridge` to `permissions.deny` in `settings.json`.

